<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Yan</h1><p class="st-meta">384 CE – 407 CE · capital Zhongshan, Longcheng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Murong Chui's restoration of the Yan state on the North China Plain, which dominated the east until the Northern Wei defeated it at Canhe Slope in 395.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a>, a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a> prince who had served <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a>, rebelled after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Fei River</a> defeat, proclaimed himself King of Yan in 384 and emperor in 386, with his capital at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhongshan">Zhongshan</a> (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingzhou">Dingzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>). He reconquered much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> heartland.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Later Yan combined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> military power with Chinese administration on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> destroyed the rival <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-yan">Western Yan</a> in 394. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-bao">Murong Bao</a>, however, led an army against the rising <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> and was crushed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-canhe-slope">Canhe Slope</a> in 395. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Chui died in 396 after a final campaign.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhongshan">Zhongshan</a> in 397, and the court retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaoyang-liaoning">Longcheng</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a>. A branch under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-de">Murong De</a> founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-yan">Southern Yan</a>. The last emperor, the extravagant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xi">Murong Xi</a>, was killed in 407 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-ba">Feng Ba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-yun">Gao Yun</a>, who founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-yan">Northern Yan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-yan.jpg" alt="Later Yan" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_391_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>384 CE – 407 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zhongshan, Longcheng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Murong Chui, Murong Bao, Murong Xi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yan (Murong Chui)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=396&amp;focus=later-yan" aria-label="Later Yan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7713 1346 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Yan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8033 1602L7769 1191M8033 1602L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8033 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8033 1602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zhongshan. <a href="/atlas?year=396&amp;focus=later-yan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
